WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:02.568
(woman)
Neil? Neil!

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.037
Yes, honey?

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.039
Neil deGrasse Tyson,
I asked you

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.440
to take the dog out
for a walk

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:10.143
when I left the house
four hours ago.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.244
Now I come back,

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.612
and this little
mother[bleep] Sputnik

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:15.048
has done pissed
on my drapes.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.616
Now I've got to get rid
of those things

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:17.817
and get new ones.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.719
Neil, it doesn't
make any sense.

00:00:19.752 --> 00:00:22.188
You are
an astrophysicist.

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:27.493
How can you not keep track
of little details like this?

00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:30.530
Well, actually,

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.165
it's the little details

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.168
that cannot be kept track of,
by definition.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:39.072
In 1927, a German
university lecturer

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.773
named Werner Heisenberg

00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:44.544
came to a seemingly
paradoxical conclusion,

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:47.146
that the more we know
about the position

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:49.215
of a particle
in physical space,

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:52.218
the less we know about
its momentum, and vice versa.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:54.087
Okay, but what--
what does that have to do--

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.189
Taken in conjunction
with what we know

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.524
about the expansion
of the universe,

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:01.828
this brings us
to a fascinating possibility,

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:06.099
that maybe what we experience
as one point in time-space

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:09.268
could actually be
a legion of points,

00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:11.771
which would mean
that your curtains

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:13.773
and little Sputnik's pee-pee

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:15.808
could be as distant
from one another

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:19.079
as we are
from the furthest galaxy.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.216
Okay.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:29.255
Well, just...
next time.

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:34.227
Of course, dear.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.829
(woman)
Neil!

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:38.598
There you are.

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:39.832
What are you doing?

00:01:39.865 --> 00:01:42.468
We have to go
to my Aunt Nelly's funeral.

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:44.804
How are you not ready?

00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:47.306
I've been talking to you
about this all week.

00:01:47.339 --> 00:01:49.242
How am I gonna find you
in your boxers

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.244
looking in
a science magazine?

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:52.311
[sighs]

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:54.247
I swear, Neil,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.548
sometimes I think

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:59.452
you don't have any idea
of what's important.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.488
Well...actually...

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:04.624
an idea of what's important

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:06.559
is as close
as we can ever come

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:09.595
to any definition
of importance.

00:02:09.628 --> 00:02:12.198
Our galaxy is one

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:15.635
of over 100 billion
in the observable universe,

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:19.672
and it's 100,000
light-years across,

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:23.876
which means it would take light
100,000 years to traverse.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:25.344
Okay, but--

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:27.580
But that's just space.

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:31.417
If we were to chart
the history of the universe

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.452
on one calendar year,

00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:36.189
the history of mankind
as we know it,

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.390
would just take place

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.392
in the final second
of that year.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:43.729
So, whether I'm ready now
or in 500 years,

00:02:43.762 --> 00:02:45.932
well, cosmically speaking,

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:48.968
the distinction is meaningless.

00:02:49.001 --> 00:02:51.271
Well...okay.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.377
I'm--I'm going now.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:01.979
Good-bye, honey.

